  "query_preview": "from the southern high plains into the upper Midwest. At\nthe surface, dry easterly flow prevailed over the Midsouth. The\nnearest upstream 12Z sounding at ILX showed extremely dry air\nbetween 850-700mb, with an average dewpoint of -25C. NAM time-\nheight section for Memphis depicted the mean RH in this layer to\nbe around 1 percent this afternoon. The base of this dry air will\nlikely partially entrain into the deepening mixed layer, which is\nforecast to reach around 5 kft this afternoon. NBM dewpoints did\nnot appear to fully capture this scenario and have been heavily\nblended with cooler dewpoints from the CONSshort for this\nafternoon. Tuesday will likely see some moderation of the extreme\ndryness near the top of the mixed layer, but surface RH will\nnonetheless prevail below 30 percent for most of the Midsouth.\nWith the upper ridge axis over the Midsouth on Tuesday, weaker\nmixed layer winds are expected, relative to today.\n\nDuring the midweek, our attention will focus on a slow-moving\nsouthern branch upper low over the desert Southwest. This feature\nwill incorporate moisture and energy from the remnants of\nHurricane Polo, as it lifts slowly east. Global model consensus\nis high regarding the evolution of the southern branch low,\nstalling it over the southern plains and weakening it\nsignificantly by Friday. Nonetheless, Midsouth rain chances\nremain on track for the weekend, aided by PWAT values near\n2 inches, the presence of a weak stationary trough axis over the\nlower Mississippi River Valley, and an approaching northern\nbranch trough axis. Given the weak deep layer shear, there will\nlikely be a diurnal trend to rain coverage and intensity, which\nwill likely reach a peak Saturday afternoon. The northern branch\ntrough axis will exit to the south and east by Sunday evening,\nwith dry midcontinental high pressure spreading into the Midsouth\nlate Sunday. Temperatures for the early part of next week should\nprevail near normal for early October.",
